    description: "FILED 2026-09-09 from INV-linub phase 1. Record:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/linub_enumeration_09092026/.\n\nINV-linub's ANALYZER STATUS block records java, scala and swift as \"emit and\nmatch in BOTH forms OK\". That verdict rests ENTIRELY on WI-nasuf's fixture\nmeasurement of 2026-08-05 -- a real pipeline and production\n_match_propagation_entry, but a FIXTURE, with an in-fixture positive control.\nIt has never been confirmed on corpus.\n\nWHY THAT IS THIN AT THESE EXPOSURES (measured 2026-09-09, all rows):\n    java    142 rows   R1-only 104   R2-only  3   dual 35   neither  0\n    scala   184 rows   R1-only 112   R2-only  5   dual 48   neither 15\n    swift   107 rows   R1-only  93   R2-only  0   dual  7   neither  7\njava's combined at-risk share is 100% of its rows and scala's is 90%. A fixture\npass at 100% exposure is the weakest evidence in the table carrying the\nstrongest claim.\n\nTHE PRECEDENT THAT MAKES THIS WORTH DOING rather than assuming: WI-nasuf's own\nkotlin work found FIVE separate defects during per-site read-back on okhttp that\nthe fixture arm had not surfaced -- nested-class keying, file-scoped var_types\nleaking across functions, chain-root constructor typing, dotted constructors at\nthe root of longer chains, and newline-bearing navigation text failing\nisidentifier(). Every one was found by reading real sites, not fixtures. There\nis no reason to expect java/scala/swift to be different in kind.\n\nSCOPE: confirm-or-refute on corpus, one repository per language. This is a\nCONFIRMATION item, and a clean result is a publishable negative that upgrades\nthree cells of INV-linub's table from fixture evidence to behavioural evidence\n-- which is what closure-evidence discipline requires before INV-linub can be\nsatisfied on those languages anyway.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE: fixing anything found. File the residual."  # 8c36
